\ headn ( dataset n leading-columns -- ), head ( dataset -- = 10 [ ] headn ):
\ print the first min(n, rows) rows as an aligned table. The leading-columns
\ symbols appear first in the given order; the remaining columns follow
\ alphabetical by name. An empty leading-columns orders every column
\ alphabetically. Column names form the header line, two-space gutter,
\ numeric/quantity right-aligned, text left-aligned, :datetime columns
\ rendered by time>iso, other cells by render.

\ === typed TSV dataset: text + numeric columns ===
"name	weight	note
fern	1.5	shade plant
oak	120	tall
moss	0.02	ground cover
ivy	3	climbs walls
" "/tmp/lf143.tsv" write-file
"/tmp/lf143.tsv" read-tsv to plants

\ === empty leading-columns: every column alphabetical (name, note, weight) ===
plants 2 [ ] headn
clear cr

\ === one leading column: weight first, the rest alphabetical (name, note) ===
plants 2 [ :weight ] headn
clear cr

\ === several leading columns in order: note, weight, then the rest (name) ===
plants 2 [ :note :weight ] headn
clear cr

\ n beyond the row count prints every row; head defaults n to 10, columns alphabetical
plants head
clear cr

\ === quantity and datetime columns, when pinned first ===
{ :when [ 1752000000 1752086400 ] vector 1 s *
  :dose [ 0.5 12 ] vector 1 kg *
  :tag [ "first" "second" ] } 5 [ :when ] headn
clear cr

\ === empty dataset: prints nothing, consumes its arguments ===
{ } 5 [ ] headn
99 . cr
